# Target C-II — host geometry removes four of the six angular-density losses

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the linear one-sided high-`t` obstruction shore through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** corrected unstrengthened good-interface fork; host-forced transverse-margin theorem; exact-antipodal exact force-pair theorem; common one-sided switch half-plane; C67 diameter-direction separation  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_host_sharpened_robust_fork_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

Let

\[
c_0={113\over20{,}000{,}000}=5.65\times10^{-6}.
\]

The corrected parent fork is

\[
|E_D|+|U_D|>c_0D-o(D),
\tag{HS.1}

where `E_D` is exact-antipodal and `U_D` is non-antipodal TB1.

The previous robust-angle theorem lost `4 rho D` from `U_D` to keep its chosen TB1 bars away from the two local opening-cone boundary rays. The host-forced theorem removes that loss pointwise: every switching diameter already lies at projective distance at least

\[
\gamma_*-o(1),
\qquad
\gamma_*:=\arcsin(0.4143)=24.47524188\ldots^\circ,
\tag{HS.2}

from the common row phase, while both local TB1 cone rays lie in the shrinking row-phase window.

Thus every member of `U_D` is automatically a uniformly strong TB1 blocker.

---

## 1. The non-antipodal side has a fixed macroscopic first-order margin

At `i in U_D`, let `a_i,b_i` be the rooted unit boundary-contact rays of the local dual cone and let `n_i` be the chosen inward switching diameter vector, `|n_i|=D`.

All projective row/contact rays lie in one window `W_D` of width `o(1)`. Hence

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([a_i],W_D)=o(1),
\qquad
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([b_i],W_D)=o(1).
\]

The host theorem gives

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([n_i],W_D)
\ge\gamma_*-o(1).
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
 d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([n_i],[a_i]),
 d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([n_i],[b_i])
 \ge\gamma_*-o(1).
}
\tag{HS.3}

Use the positive-cone orientation from the TB1 theorem. If `t_i` is the ordinary angular position of `n_i` inside the local dual cone of width `theta_i`, then

\[
t_i,\theta_i-t_i\ge\gamma_*-o(1).
\tag{HS.4}

For every unit local contact-opening tangent `w`, the same elementary cone calculation as in the strong-TB1 theorem yields

\[
\boxed{
 n_i\cdot w
 \ge D\sin(\gamma_*-o(1))
 =(0.4143-o(1))D.
}
\tag{HS.5}

No C67 deletion is used. Up to the harmless finite-source angular errors,

\[
\boxed{S_D^{host}=U_D.}
\tag{HS.6}

---

## 2. Only the exact-antipodal pair-gap extraction still costs density

At every `i in E_D`, choose the opposite-transverse pair that positively generates the exact reaction `G_i`. Let its projective gap be `eta_i`.

The existing narrow-pair capacity proof applies unchanged: it uses the common one-sided open semicircle, the shrinking row-phase window, opposite transverse signs, disjoint switching supports across interfaces, and C67 separation. Therefore for every fixed `rho>0`,

\[
\boxed{
\#\{i\in E_D:\eta_i<\rho\}
\le2\rho D+o(D).
}
\tag{HS.7}

Put

\[
E_D^{force}(\rho)=\{i\in E_D:\eta_i\ge\rho\}.
\]

Combining (HS.1), (HS.6), and (HS.7) gives, for every fixed

\[
0<\rho<{c_0\over2},
\]

\[
\boxed{
|S_D^{host}|+|E_D^{force}(\rho)|
>(c_0-2\rho)D-o(D).
}
\tag{HS.8}

Hence one mechanism has population

\[
\boxed{
>{c_0-2\rho\over2}D-o(D).
}
\tag{HS.9}

On the TB1 side the opening margin is always `(0.4143-o(1))D`, independent of `rho`. On the exact side the two force rays have pair gap at least `rho` and each individually stays at least `gamma_*-o(1)` from the reaction/row axis.

This replaces the old `(c_0-6rho)` combined population by `(c_0-2rho)` and expands the admissible range from `rho<c_0/6` to `rho<c_0/2`.

---

## 3. Improved deep exact-force density

The deep exact-force branch is the branch on which no positive-density fixed-margin TB1 population survives. Section 1 then forces

\[
|U_D|=o(D).
\tag{HS.10}

The parent fork (HS.1) gives

\[
|E_D|>c_0D-o(D).
\tag{HS.11}

Fix a pair-gap parameter `rho>0` and a separator-neighborhood loss `eta>0`. Removing `rho`-narrow force pairs costs at most `2rho D+o(D)`. The radius-`r` separator-neighborhood deletion costs `eta D+o(D)` after choosing the local complexity threshold as in the parameterized deep-grid theorem. Bilateral birth removes only `O_rho(1)` centers. The individual transverse margin requires no deletion.

Therefore, whenever

\[
c_0-2\rho-\eta>0,
\]

there are more than

\[
\boxed{
(c_0-2\rho-\eta)D-o(D)
}
\tag{HS.12}

deep bilateral exact-force centers with fixed pair gap `rho`, fixed individual host margin `gamma_*-o(1)`, exact positive force compression, the common inverse-`D` train grid, and any prescribed finite exact-phase depth. The usual diagonal extraction lets the depth tend to infinity with the same leading density.

For the displayed choices

\[
\rho={c_0\over100},
\qquad
\eta={c_0\over50},
\]

this becomes

\[
\boxed{0.96c_0=5.424\times10^{-6}.}
\tag{HS.13}

The earlier two-sided family at the same nominal parameter scale had coefficient `0.90c_0=5.085e-6`.

---

## 4. Useful calibrated choices

If a later exact-antipodal contradiction pays one power of the pair gap, the guaranteed product

\[
\rho{c_0-2\rho\over2}
\]

is maximized at

\[
\boxed{\rho={c_0\over4}.}
\tag{HS.14}

At that scale one mechanism has more than

\[
\boxed{{c_0\over4}D-o(D)=1.4125\times10^{-6}D-o(D).}
\tag{HS.15}

If a later argument pays the square of the pair gap, the corresponding product is maximized at

\[
\boxed{\rho={c_0\over3},}
\tag{HS.16}

leaving one mechanism with more than

\[
\boxed{{c_0\over6}D-o(D)=9.416666\ldots\times10^{-7}D-o(D).}
\tag{HS.17}

Again the TB1 margin remains the fixed host-geometric value, not `rho`.

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The high-`t` local obstruction now has the quantitative form

```text
for every fixed 0 < rho < c0/2,

  rho-wide exact force-carrying antipodal pairs
  OR
  host-forced TB1 blockers with >=(0.4143-o(1))D opening margin

occupy together > (c0-2rho)D-o(D) interfaces.
```

On the branch without positive-density strong TB1, almost the whole original `c_0D` good-interface population is exact-antipodal before the `2rho` pair-gap and `eta` deep-neighborhood losses.

This does not close C-II. It removes the dominant `4rho` robustness loss and makes the non-antipodal side uniformly strong at a macroscopic angle fixed by the physical host arcs.

**Disposition:** host geometry upgrades every non-antipodal TB1 interface to a fixed-margin blocker, improves the robust population from `c_0-6rho` to `c_0-2rho`, and improves the deep exact-force density to `c_0-2rho-eta`.